<edge source="us_china_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="allied_arms_reshaping::exec_directive_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Tighter US export controls create immediate compliance and interoperability gaps that prompt allied executives to issue frequent, forceful directives reshaping military policy and acquisition rules to secure access and legal cover.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">allied_arms_reshaping</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="iran_hormuz_standoff::maritime_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Sharpened US/Western export controls on Chinese high tech push Beijing toward closer operational and diplomatic alignment with Iran and encourage Iranian and proxy harassment of vessels associated with sanctioning states, raising the frequency of collisions, seizures and interdictions in the Strait.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="iran_hormuz_standoff::oil_export_strait" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating export controls that heighten strategic tensions provoke Iranian signaling (threats, interdictions, temporary closures) intended to deter sanction enforcement, which directly lowers the volume of oil able or willing to transit the Strait.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="israel_lebanon_conflict::diplomatic_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.62</data>
  <data key="d37">Sharper U.S. export controls provoke Beijing and Moscow to escalate public diplomacy and mediatory engagement with Lebanon, Iran and UN fora as a counterweight to U.S. policy, increasing external diplomatic interventions around the Israel–Lebanon front.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="russia_ukraine_blacksea::drone_strike_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Tighter US export controls on advanced semiconductors and dual‑use components reduce China's ability to source or re‑export critical components to Russian drone and munition producers, degrading Russian production/maintenance and lowering the frequency of drone/missile strikes on maritime targets.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_blacksea</data>
</edge>
