<edge source="israel_lebanon_conflict::diplomatic_pressure" target="allied_arms_reshaping::exec_directive_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened diplomatic démarches and allied demands prompt executives to issue more frequent and forceful directives (export approvals, deployment authorizations, sanctions waivers or new ROE guidance) to signal responsiveness and manage partner relations.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">allied_arms_reshaping</data>
</edge>
<edge source="israel_lebanon_conflict::diplomatic_pressure" target="iran_hormuz_standoff::oil_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Intensified external diplomatic pressure aimed at de‑escalating Israeli–Lebanese hostilities reduces the perceived risk of a broader regional conflagration and thus cuts the risk premium baked into global oil prices, lowering the benchmark level.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="israel_lebanon_conflict::diplomatic_pressure" target="us_china_rivalry::export_control_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased external diplomatic pressure tied to the conflict leads the US and partner governments to coordinate tighter sanctions and expand export‑control lists for dual‑use goods (to prevent diversion to regional proxies), broadening the scope and enforcement of controls that also target technology flows to China.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_rivalry</data>
</edge>
