  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="israel_lebanon_conflict::crossborder_incidents" target="allied_arms_reshaping::deployment_tempo" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Episodes of cross-border strikes or incursions prompt allied governments and coalition commanders to accelerate and re-route unit rotations, naval patrols and air assets into the theater as an immediate deterrent and force-protection measure.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">allied_arms_reshaping</data>
</edge>
<edge source="israel_lebanon_conflict::crossborder_incidents" target="iran_hormuz_standoff::maritime_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained increases in cross‑border strikes (e.g., Israeli strikes on Hezbollah positions) provoke Tehran to authorize or task naval and proxy actors to attack or harass shipping in the Persian Gulf as asymmetric retaliation, raising the incidence of maritime incidents in the Strait of Hormuz.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="israel_lebanon_conflict::crossborder_incidents" target="russia_ukraine_blacksea::maritime_throughput" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher frequency of cross‑border incidents raises perceived risk for commercial shipping and insurers across adjacent sea lanes, prompting carriers to reduce sailings or reroute away from the region and thereby cutting vessel transits and cargo throughput connected to the Black Sea corridor.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_blacksea</data>
</edge>
<edge source="israel_lebanon_conflict::crossborder_incidents" target="us_china_rivalry::supply_diversification" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in cross‑border incidents around Israel and Lebanon causes short‑term disruptions and insurance/shipping reroutes through the eastern Mediterranean and Red Sea, prompting US firms and allies to shift procurement and logistics away from vulnerable chokepoints and concentrated suppliers and thereby accelerate supplier and route diversification.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_lebanon_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_rivalry</data>
</edge>
